## Building ARX-01

Before manufacturing the board:

1. Read the current `ARX-01_Preliminary_Datasheet.pdf`.
2. Review the design files contained in `ARX_4.1.zip`.
3. Check the BOM for component availability and substitutions.
4. Verify PCB manufacturing requirements.
5. Verify component orientation and placement against the design files.
6. Review the current hardware revision before ordering PCBs or assembled boards.

This is an active open-source hardware project. Design files and specifications may change as the receiver is developed and characterized.

## Using ARX-01 in an Optical Communications System

A complete free-space optical communications system normally includes more than the receiver front end.

A simplified receive path is:

**Optical transmitter -> free-space optical channel -> collection optics -> photodetector / ARX-01 receiver front end -> signal processing -> PPM/OOK demodulation -> recovered data**

System performance depends on many parameters beyond the receiver itself, including:

* Transmitter optical power
* Transmit optics
* Receiver aperture
* Optical wavelength
* Beam divergence
* Pointing accuracy
* Atmospheric loss
* Background light
* Detector characteristics
* Receiver bandwidth
* Modulation format
* PPM order
* Pulse width
* Symbol rate
* Detection threshold
* Error-correction coding
* Signal-processing implementation

Users designing complete links should perform an optical link budget for their intended application.

## Pulse Position Modulation

Pulse Position Modulation encodes information according to the location of an optical pulse within a defined symbol interval.

For an M-ary PPM system, each symbol contains multiple possible pulse positions, with the selected position carrying information.

PPM is commonly considered for power-limited and photon-limited optical communications because it can provide high peak-to-average optical power and good photon efficiency.

ARX-01 is intended to provide a practical open-hardware receiver front end for experimentation with these types of optical links.

## On-Off Keying

ARX-01 can also be used in optical systems employing On-Off Keying.

In a basic OOK link, the presence or absence of optical energy represents transmitted information.

OOK can be useful for simpler optical communication systems, initial link testing, hardware characterization, and applications where the additional timing complexity of PPM is unnecessary.

## Open-Source Optical Communications

One goal of this project is to reduce the barrier to experimenting with real optical communication hardware.

Much of the equipment used for high-performance free-space optical and laser communications is specialized or proprietary. Publishing the receiver design, manufacturing information, and technical documentation makes it possible for others to:

* Build the hardware
* Characterize it independently
* Modify the design
* Develop compatible optical transmitters
* Develop PPM and OOK demodulators
* Experiment with coding and synchronization
* Perform optical link tests
* Use the design for education and research
* Contribute improvements back to the project

If you build or modify ARX-01, documenting your results can help the wider open-source optical communications community.

